<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The input method's own metadata, pointed at by the `android.view.im` meta-data
  on ParleyInputMethodService in AndroidManifest.xml. The system reads this to
  build the keyboard list, so the label and the settings link here are what the
  user sees in Settings → System → Languages & input.

  `settingsActivity` is what makes the "Parley" row in that list tappable. It is
  also the only place a runtime permission can be asked for on the whole
  voice-typing path, because an InputMethodService has no Activity — see
  VoiceTypingSettingsActivity.

  `supportsSwitchingToNextInputMethod` is what allows the pane's keyboard key to
  call switchToNextInputMethod(false). Parley is voice-only and has no character
  keys, so handing typing back to whatever the user was using before is not a
  courtesy, it is the other half of the feature; without this flag the call is
  refused and the user is stranded on a keyboard that cannot type.

  One subtype, mode "voice". That is the declaration that tells the platform this
  is a dictation input method rather than a layout — it is how the system's own
  voice typing is declared, and it is what lets Android place the keyboard
  correctly in pickers instead of listing it among the layouts. iOS has to draw
  its own side-slide track between panels to achieve the same thing.

  The subtype is deliberately not locale-pinned. Parley sends the relay
  `language_hints = ["zh", "en"]` for every dictation (DictationSession) and the
  provider detects which is being spoken, so declaring a locale per subtype would
  advertise a choice the pipeline does not actually make — and would put two
  indistinguishable Parley rows in the user's keyboard switcher.
-->
<input-method
    xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:settingsActivity="com.pathors.parley.ime.VoiceTypingSettingsActivity"
    android:supportsSwitchingToNextInputMethod="true">
    <subtype
        android:label="@string/ime_subtype_voice"
        android:imeSubtypeMode="voice"
        android:isAsciiCapable="false" />
</input-method>
